Hi everyone -

This mailing list is great for communicating ideas and problems, but when it comes to sharing code examples, it can be a bit tedious for app authors to cut and paste their files into an email message, and it can be even more tedious for readers to re-create the files from the email message.

I've written a set of functions that makes this easier:
shinySerialize() turns your Shiny app into text that can be cut-and-pasted into an email message.
shinyDeserialize() does the reverse: it turns that text into a set of files, in an application directory (and subdirectories, if needed).
shinyRun() is a wrapper function that first calls shinyDeserialize() to write out the app, then calls runApp() to run it.

These functions aren't part of the Shiny package; for now they're just things I've been playing with. They're defined here:
https://gist.github.com/wch/5003044

You can load these functions by simply sourcing the file at this URL, using the downloader package:

install.packages('downloader')

# Load the functions
downloader::source_url("https://gist.github.com/wch/5003044/raw/aa4dbbca0023e29a629386b1ee9a5d27b2e37362/shinyserialize.r")

Once you've done that, the functions will be available for you to use.

To convert a Shiny app to a format that can be easily copied and pasted, run:

shinySerialize('myapp_dir/')

It will output something like this, which you can copy and paste into an email, and other can copy and paste to run (after sourcing the URL above):

shinyRun(
#--------------------------------------------------------------
`server.R` = quote({
shinyServer(function(input, output) {
    output$distPlot <- reactivePlot(function() {
        dist <- rnorm(input$obs)
        hist(dist)
    })
})
}),
#--------------------------------------------------------------
`ui.R` = quote({
shinyUI(pageWithSidebar(
  headerPanel("Hello Shiny!"),
  sidebarPanel(sliderInput("obs",
    "Number of observations:", min = 0, max = 1000, value = 500)),
    mainPanel(plotOutput("distPlot"))
))
})
)

When you distribute your code in this format, put the call to source_url() at the top, then put the call to shinyRun() after that. So if you send an email to the list with a simple example, you'd do something like this:

downloader::source_url("https://gist.github.com/wch/5003044/raw/aa4dbbca0023e29a629386b1ee9a5d27b2e37362/shinyserialize.r",
  sha="62bbd827ac073e9ad41dacb1e498b64b5c4659df")

shinyRun(
#--------------------------------------------------------------
`server.R` = quote({
shinyServer(function(input, output) {
    output$distPlot <- reactivePlot(function() {
        dist <- rnorm(input$obs)
        hist(dist)
    })
})
}),
#--------------------------------------------------------------
`ui.R` = quote({
shinyUI(pageWithSidebar(
  headerPanel("Hello Shiny!"),
  sidebarPanel(sliderInput("obs",
    "Number of observations:", min = 0, max = 1000, value = 500)),
    mainPanel(plotOutput("distPlot"))
))
})
)

If you want to simple write out the files to a directory, you can replace shinyRun withshinyDeserialize, with something like this:

shinyDeserialize(appdir="my_outputdir",
  `server.R` = ...,
  `ui.R` = ...
)

Hopefully this will make it easier to share ideas. Enjoy!

-Winston

